WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.540
How can the same light touch recruit a pain pathway without changing the skin

2
00:00:04.540 --> 00:00:09.280
stimulus? Watch one blue touch signal enter the dorsal horn.

3
00:00:10.480 --> 00:00:15.200
It meets an orange relay, a purple inhibitory brake, and a red output route.

4
00:00:18.633 --> 00:00:19.993
First test the relay.

5
00:00:21.273 --> 00:00:27.513
In mice, removing the mostly excitatory somatostatin lineage weakens acute and

6
00:00:27.513 --> 00:00:30.373
injury-associated mechanical nocifensive behaviour.

7
00:00:31.773 --> 00:00:34.833
The result supports a relay role in the assays tested.

8
00:00:36.033 --> 00:00:39.333
It does not identify every route for mechanical pain.

9
00:00:40.383 --> 00:00:43.253
Now restore the relay and test its brake.

10
00:00:43.253 --> 00:00:49.063
Dynorphin-lineage inhibitory neurons restrict recruitment of superficial

11
00:00:49.063 --> 00:00:53.603
somatostatin neurons by large myelinated afferents.

12
00:00:54.203 --> 00:00:55.683
The blue input is unchanged.

13
00:00:57.103 --> 00:01:01.623
Removing purple inhibition lets that input recruit many more orange cells.

14
00:01:02.733 --> 00:01:04.413
Predict the slice result.

15
00:01:05.673 --> 00:01:11.073
With the inhibitory population removed, does identical Aβ input recruit fewer

16
00:01:11.073 --> 00:01:15.033
superficial somatostatin cells, the same number, or more?

17
00:01:16.333 --> 00:01:18.613
Keep the incoming pulse fixed while you decide.

18
00:01:24.600 --> 00:01:32.760
More. At the lamina II–III border, Aβ-evoked spiking rose from 5% to 40% percent of

19
00:01:32.760 --> 00:01:37.300
recorded neurons. The measurement locates a gated connection.

20
00:01:38.620 --> 00:01:41.680
The behaviour alone could not tell us where the circuit failed.

21
00:01:43.183 --> 00:01:44.543
Tools set the claim.

22
00:01:45.903 --> 00:01:50.643
Ablation removes captured cells, including cells marked by earlier expression.

23
00:01:52.123 --> 00:01:56.423
Tetanus toxin light chain can leave a neuron alive while blocking transmitter

24
00:01:56.423 --> 00:02:02.183
release. Activation asks whether added output can shift the measured behaviour.

25
00:02:03.383 --> 00:02:06.563
Opposite manipulations sharpen the relay claim.

26
00:02:07.983 --> 00:02:11.783
Somatostatin activation elicited nocifensive behaviour.

27
00:02:13.223 --> 00:02:18.083
Inhibition raised mechanical withdrawal thresholds and lengthened thermal withdrawal

28
00:02:18.083 --> 00:02:19.803
latency in naive mice.

29
00:02:21.383 --> 00:02:25.503
Slice recordings confirmed that the light-driven neurons followed the stimulus

30
00:02:25.503 --> 00:02:26.503
trains.

31
00:02:27.167 --> 00:02:32.847
After spared nerve injury, permanent reporter counts of parvalbumin neurons did not

32
00:02:32.847 --> 00:02:36.207
fall significantly at 3, 5, or 8 weeks.

33
00:02:37.407 --> 00:02:41.887
Yet activating those neurons relieved injury-associated hypersensitivity.

34
00:02:43.407 --> 00:02:44.407
The cells remained.

35
00:02:45.607 --> 00:02:48.107
The next question is what happened to their signal.

36
00:02:49.150 --> 00:02:52.430
Inhibitory populations are not interchangeable.

37
00:02:53.990 --> 00:02:59.330
Parvalbumin activation changed mechanical sensitivity without changing thermal

38
00:02:59.330 --> 00:03:01.430
sensitivity in the assays tested.

39
00:03:02.850 --> 00:03:08.270
Broader glycinergic manipulations changed mechanical, heat, and cold sensitivity

40
00:03:08.270 --> 00:03:12.550
together. One gate does not control every modality.

41
00:03:13.633 --> 00:03:16.573
So begin with behaviour, but do not stop there.

42
00:03:17.813 --> 00:03:21.933
Ask which cells were captured, what the tool changed, and which connection was

43
00:03:21.933 --> 00:03:25.713
measured. Behaviour identifies a failed gate.

44
00:03:27.273 --> 00:03:29.153
Circuit measurements help locate the failure.
